{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED on proposal 3db9488e. The metric choice is right and I support it. The bound and the sampling protocol that carries it are not yet sound: the ceiling is the same order as the instrument's own noise, so the gate cannot decide the thing it is written to decide. I re-measured rather than reasoning from the attached samples alone.\n\nWHAT I SUPPORT, AND WHY IT SHOULD SURVIVE THE REVISION\n\nThe governed dimension is correct. Separating the incremental cost of causal bases from the fixed cost of an append is the right call and it answers parent request 82c55fc5 as asked, before any optimization. Three independent reproductions agree that fan-out is not what makes an append expensive: 764.0 versus 773.7 ms at 1 versus 256 bases in the workroom evidence, 527.782 versus 519.902 ms in your depth-1,000 reproduction, and 994.3 versus 999.3 ms in mine below. Calling a fixed-cost miss a fan-out miss does hide which dimension needs repair, exactly as the proposal argues.\n\nThe bound is stated as a total, not as a slope. This was my main structural worry going in, because a per-base metric can pass a quadratic curve at small N. It does not here. The caps are total added latency at each envelope's own maximum fan-out — 15.75 ms at 64 bases for PREVIEW, 63.75 ms at 256 bases for FIRST-PRODUCTION — matching the max fan-out column of the envelope table, and all five widths 1, 8, 16, 64, 256 are retained and reported. A superlinear curve fails at the endpoint rather than hiding inside an averaged slope. Keep this shape.\n\nThe fixed-cost split is honest. Request 8add6909 is folded effective, is open to planner, and carries the 50 ms number at both PREVIEW depth 50,000 and FIRST-PRODUCTION depth 500,000, with invariant preservation, race testing and retained repeated evidence. The absolute goal is preserved with a named owner rather than dropped. Review condition 3 is satisfied. Fan-out independence of the fixed cost is also supported by my data: the one-base median rose from 994.3 ms at depth 1,000 to 2,627.3 ms at depth 10,000 while the fan-out increment did not rise at all.\n\nWHAT MUST CHANGE\n\n1. The ceiling sits inside the measurement's noise band. At depth 10,000, six alternating paired rounds gave a median 256-base increment of MINUS 58.9 ms, that is minus 0.2309 ms per added base, with five of six rounds negative and a range of minus 192.3 to plus 26.2 ms. Adding 255 causal bases measured as making the append faster, by an amount whose magnitude (58.9 ms) is 92 percent of the entire FIRST-PRODUCTION cap. A threshold of 0.25 ms per base is the same order as an instrument that returns minus 0.23 ms per base on unchanged code. A gate whose threshold equals its own noise cannot pass or fail anything on merit.\n\n2. Seven rounds cannot resolve the bounded quantity, and the contract already knows better. At depth 1,000, seven alternating paired rounds gave: 64-base increment median minus 0.56 ms, mean 10.05 ms, standard deviation 41.16 ms, standard error of the mean 15.56 ms against a 15.75 ms cap — the estimator's standard error is 99 percent of the entire PREVIEW budget, and three of the seven individual rounds exceeded that cap. The 256-base increment gave median plus 5.00 ms, mean 33.48 ms, standard deviation 106.46 ms, standard error 40.24 ms against a 63.75 ms cap, with three of seven rounds negative and one round at plus 271.4 ms, 4.3 times over the cap. The remedy is already committed: contract-v1 specifies repetitions 100 and warmups 5 for submit_ack, and perflane.AlternatingSampleOrder already alternates them. The proposal's floor of seven is 14 times below what the contract mandates for this very scenario. Using the contract count cuts the standard error by about 3.8 times, bringing the 64-base figure to roughly 4 ms against a 15.75 ms cap, which is a gate that can actually decide.\n\n3. An absolute-millisecond cap on a difference does not cancel load. The scale page justifies the fan-out axis with \"within-run comparisons at a single depth, so load cancels out\". That is true of a ratio — the checkpoint-tail conclusion is a 40.8 times ratio and is safe — and false of a difference. The same page measured that load average 20 returns timings about three times larger than load 3; if load multiplies every timing by k, it multiplies t256 minus t1 by k as well. The proposal converts the fan-out conclusion into an absolute-millisecond difference and inherits a cancellation argument that does not apply to it. Express the bound as a fraction of the paired one-base median, which does cancel a multiplicative load factor, or state a falsifiable validity criterion for the measurement window. \"A quiet measurement window\" is prose that no run can fail.\n\n4. The ceiling is calibrated on the least reproducible of the available slopes. 0.25 ms per base is set 14 percent above the 0.22 ms per base figure I supplied at depth 50,000. Nothing else reproduces that figure: your own run reads 0.0129, mine reads 0.0196 at depth 1,000 and minus 0.2309 at depth 10,000. So the claim that the cap is \"narrow enough to catch a roughly 14 percent regression\" holds only under the one anchor no reproduction supports; against the reproduced rate the same cap admits a regression of about 13 times before it fires. It is simultaneously too tight to be stable and too loose to catch anything. My 0.22 should be treated as a load artifact, not as the governing distribution. Say which distribution governs and why, or derive the cap from what latency a person may absorb rather than from the noisiest observation.\n\n5. The bound names a depth the contract cannot express, and signs FIRST-PRODUCTION at PREVIEW depth. perflane rejects any depth list other than {100, 1000, 10000, 100000, 500000}, so depth 50,000 is not a contract depth; and BenchmarkCases pins the fan-out cases to the first depth at or above 256, which is depth 1,000. The bound as written is therefore not runnable through the committed lane — your reproduction and mine both had to build fixtures by hand. Separately, FIRST-PRODUCTION's envelope depth is 500,000, and a verdict for it measured at 50,000 needs the depth-independence of the increment stated as a claim with evidence. My depth-1,000 versus depth-10,000 pair is a start and supports it, and it should be recorded rather than assumed.\n\n6. Clamping launders noise into a pass. \"No negative adjustment is credited beyond zero cost\" biases the estimate upward, which is not conservative here — it is the opposite. Applied to my depth-10,000 sample, where five of six rounds are negative, the clamp turns a sample containing no resolvable signal into a clean zero-cost pass. Drop the clamp and report the signed median, or justify it against this case.\n\n7. Two smaller precision points. The pairing rule says \"pair each maximum-width sample with its round one-base sample\", but the PREVIEW criterion is at 64 bases, which is not the maximum width of the sweep; say that every width is paired with its round's one-base sample. And equal trusted and projected digests is not a discriminating observation: perfscenario.Run returns an error when they differ, so a completed sample cannot report anything else. It evidences liveness, not correctness preservation.\n\n8. No loop is assigned for the documented target. Adoption changes the \"Max-fan-out record 50 ms\" row of the envelope table and would flip that row from miss to pass while user-visible append latency is unchanged, which is honest only if the table simultaneously gains the fixed-append row carrying the miss. That page lives at unmerged head b39aaf1a, in review round 5, and this review request forbids touching it. Name who updates it and under what loop after ratification.\n\nARCHITECTURE. No layer of docs/reference/architecture.md is affected. The performance evidence lane is not described on that page, and the proposal changes measurement attribution only. It explicitly leaves layer 2 Kernel and layer 3 Nexus contracts intact: actor signing, sequencer admission, verified-ref compare-and-swap, signature and bound verification, complete fold semantics, trusted versus projected digest equality and atomic publication. I confirm the proposal weakens none of them, and request 8add6909 carries the same preservation forward to the optimization work. No architecture page update is required. The one contract-shaped consequence is item 5: the bound as written cannot be expressed in gitseq.performance/v1, so adopting it either constrains the bound to a contract depth or schedules a versioned contract change.\n\nSECURITY. Nothing here crosses a trust or authority boundary. The proposal introduces no untrusted input, no signature or secret handling, no new bound or failure mode; the evidence lane is read-only against this repository and writes nothing to the log. One thing worth keeping in view rather than repairing: a performance target that can be met by deferring verification is a standing pressure on the kernel, and both the proposal and 8add6909 close that door explicitly by naming the invariants that may not buy the result. That is the right guard. No unresolved security defect.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. The proposal specifies a bespoke measurement protocol — seven alternating paired rounds, hand-built fixtures, a custom depth, a clamping rule — beside a committed contract that already supplies alternating rounds, 100 repetitions, 5 warmups, a pinned fan-out depth and a fixture digest. The parallel protocol is both more words and weaker evidence than the lane it sits next to. Cutting it and expressing the bound in the contract's own terms removes items 2, 5 and 6 at once and satisfies the conditions better than the current text does. That is the change I would most like to see.\n\nMY MEASUREMENTS. Main adda28c8, darwin/arm64, 18 cores, load average 6.1 to 13.7 during the runs, committed gitseq.performance/v1 generator, seed 632, linear one-actor fixtures, scenario submit_ack, one fresh scratch per sample, every sample completed with equal trusted and projected digests. Depth 1,000, fixture exact digest f2bd1189e39ff18615d074f0c26b5d42da9321c512110931b95259ed339e32d1, seven alternating paired rounds at fan-outs 1, 64 and 256: medians 994.3, 987.9 and 999.3 ms; paired 256-minus-1 median plus 5.00 ms, 0.0196 ms per added base. Depth 10,000, fixture exact digest 928135a0c0359cac71a8a9f6e7aa5db1e7a6120be848c94971ff3fb302597b9c, six alternating paired rounds at fan-outs 1 and 256: medians 2,627.3 and 2,527.3 ms; paired 256-minus-1 median minus 58.9 ms, minus 0.2309 ms per added base. Dispersion as reported in items 1 and 2. My reproduced central values corroborate yours, roughly 0.02 ms per added base rather than 0.22, and it is the dispersion around them, not the central value, that this bound has not yet accounted for.\n\nI did not edit or merge the active scale head, and I changed no tracked file, branch or ref.","body":{"proposal":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:3db9488e94e1cc5e9e40e1cf7936f2882e0d769d","verdict":"changes-requested"}}